\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{RL for multi-turn LLM agents.}
GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}, PPO~\cite{schulman2017ppo}, and RLHF methods~\cite{ouyang2022instructgpt,rafailov2023dpo} have been extended to multi-turn agents~\cite{yao2023react,ragen2025} via step-independent windowing. Subsequent work improves algorithms~\cite{feng2025gigpo,dapo2025}, rewards~\cite{selfguide2026}, or pipelines~\cite{spear2025,digiRL2024}, but all assume windowing is benign.

\paragraph{Credit assignment.}
Classical methods span bootstrapping~\cite{sutton1988td} to Monte Carlo~\cite{williams1992reinforce,schulman2016gae} (Appendix~\ref{app:classical_connection}). Step-independent GRPO inherits the worst of outcome-vs-process supervision~\cite{lightman2024lets}: coarse credit without correct direction. ArCHer~\cite{zhou2024archer}, VinePPO~\cite{kazemnejad2024vineppo}, PRIME~\cite{prime2025}, and VAPO~\cite{vapo2025} improve credit granularity; our analysis explains when such methods are safe (only after M2 is resolved). IRC~\cite{irc2026}, MC-GRPO~\cite{mcgrpo2026}, GRPO-$\lambda$~\cite{grpolambda2025}, and OAR~\cite{oar2026} corroborate our sign-level diagnosis. Turn-PPO~\cite{turnppo2025}, GAGPO~\cite{gagpo2026}, MT-GRPO~\cite{mtgrpo2025}, SWEET-RL~\cite{sweetrl2025}, iStar~\cite{istar2025}, and HCAPO~\cite{hcapo2026} propose finer-grained credit that addresses sign correctness.

\paragraph{GRPO collapse phenomena.}
RAGEN-2~\cite{ragen2_2026}, GRPO-Collapse~\cite{grpocollapse2025}, RC-GRPO~\cite{rcgrpo2026}, and EDGE-GRPO~\cite{edgegrpo2025} address variance collapse. Our dual-mechanism diagnosis unifies these: reward variance collapse is a special case of reward saturation (Section~\ref{sec:theory}), and the death spiral follows from the M1--M2 vicious cycle.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Step-independent training fails through two mechanisms: information loss imposes a constant performance ceiling while sign misattribution causes cumulative degradation. Episode-level GRPO validates the diagnosis by addressing both, though only 36\% of seeds achieve significant OOD generalization. This finding applies to GRPO-style methods under step-level windowing, sparse reward, and group-relative normalization (Section~\ref{sec:dual_mechanism}), verified on GRPO in ALFWorld and ScienceWorld.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Generality.}
Sign misattribution arises whenever three properties co-occur: step-level windowing, sparse binary reward, and group-relative normalization---theoretically sufficient within any group-relative framework (Section~\ref{sec:theory}), though verified only on GRPO with ALFWorld and ScienceWorld. M1 and M2 interact: M1 lowers $p$, increasing wrong-sign updates under M2, which further degrades $p$ (Appendix~\ref{app:why_defaults}).

\paragraph{Mechanism interaction and seed dependence.}
Cell~C (full trajectory + step-level advantage) produces NaN instability rather than gradual collapse, suggesting M1 may unintentionally stabilize training by limiting gradient variance. Multi-seed evaluation reveals bimodal OOD transfer---with training duration unable to explain the gap (seed100: $+$1.5pp step~5$\to$10), pointing to initialization sensitivity (Appendix~\ref{app:early_signal}).

\paragraph{Concurrent methods.}
HCAPO~\cite{hcapo2026} and GiGPO~\cite{feng2025gigpo} succeed with the same $h{=}2$ that causes Cell~A collapse by replacing step-level advantage with finer-grained credit, directly eliminating M2 while preserving M1. Our framework provides a consistent post-hoc explanation: HCAPO's 91.4\% (7B) and GiGPO's 90.2\% are consistent with M2 being the primary bottleneck at sufficient scale, where M1's ceiling permits strong performance. We emphasize these are post-hoc consistency analyses based on published results, not predictions; we do not reproduce these methods (see also Limitations). A practical decision rule follows: diagnose which mechanism is active, then select the fix (Appendix~\ref{app:method_selection}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Step-independent training collapses while episode-level training succeeds; a partial factorial design separates the two mechanisms; and window size $h$ modulates severity.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate on ALFWorld~\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment with six task types requiring sequential reasoning over up to 50 steps. The environment provides ternary reward: $R \in \{r_-, 0, r_+\}$, where $r_- = -0.1$ per invalid action, $0$ for valid but unsuccessful actions, and $r_+$ for task success. Our theoretical analysis (Section~\ref{sec:theory}) assumes the simplified binary case ($R \in \{0, r_+\}$), which yields an upper bound on sign misattribution severity; Section~\ref{sec:sign_verification} validates this bound under both reward structures. The base policy is Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned on expert demonstrations (SFT baseline: 12.5\% in-distribution). At this baseline success rate, Proposition~2 predicts the majority of gradient updates carry incorrect sign. All conditions use RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025} (v0.1, commit \texttt{3a6b48b})\footnote{Our implementation extends RAGEN v0.1 with episode-level training support. Code available upon acceptance.} with GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} ($K{=}8$; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparameters}). We report success rate on a 134-task OOD set (primary) and 32-task in-distribution set (secondary). Seed counts are noted per condition.

\subsection{Partial Factorial Mechanism Separation}
\label{sec:factorial_experiments}

We implement the partial factorial design from Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design} (3 of 4 cells; Cell~C excluded due to NaN instability) with two seeds per condition. Cell~C (full observation, step-level advantage) would isolate M2 without M1, but we encountered persistent training instability in this configuration (Appendix~\ref{app:cell_c}). Cell~C's NaN divergence, qualitatively different from Cell~A's gradual collapse, suggests that information loss may have an unintended stabilizing effect on gradient variance, and that the M1--M2 interaction may be non-additive (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cell~A collapses in two phases (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}): task success drops to 0\% while action validity initially remains high, then continued sign misattribution destroys even basic formatting. % exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, hsweep_h2_seed2
Cell~B eliminates sign misattribution while retaining information loss: training success improves, but OOD generalization (5.97\%) remains near the SFT baseline (6.72\%), confirming that removing M2 alone bounds learning without causing degradation.% exp_id: factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed2
Cell~D is the only condition that substantially exceeds the SFT baseline, with monotonic OOD improvement across both seeds.% exp_id: exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_ood_v2
The A$\to$B comparison isolates M2; the B$\to$D comparison isolates M1. Both mechanisms are necessary to explain the full failure pattern (Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}a).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_training_dynamics.pdf}
\caption{(a)~Training dynamics across factorial conditions (metric type indicated in legend). Cell~A collapses within 5 steps; Cell~B shows bounded learning; Cell~D improves monotonically. (b)~Window size $h$ vs.\ success rate (single seed). $h{\leq}20$ bars use a 32-task in-distribution eval set; the episode-level bar uses a 134-task OOD set (dotted separator); magnitudes are not directly comparable across eval sets. A single-seed observation suggests a credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$. Hatched bars indicate collapsed conditions.}
\label{fig:hsweep}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{1.7B results on ALFWorld (OOD 134-task set, In-Dist 32-task set). All cells report best checkpoint per seed. Cell~A: 2 seeds, best checkpoint each (both 0\%). Cell~B: 2 seeds, best checkpoint each. Cell~D: 2 seeds, mean $\pm$ range. Cell~D mean OOD (17.2\%) is consistent with the 11-seed evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability}). Full seed-level breakdown in Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} (11 seeds) and Appendix Table~\ref{tab:additional_baselines}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Mechanisms} & \textbf{Best Step} & \textbf{In-Dist (\%)} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & --- & --- & 12.50 & 6.72 \\
Cell A ($h{=}2$, step) & M1+M2 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\
Cell B ($h{=}2$, episode) & M1 only & 5 & 18.75 & 5.97 \\
Cell D (full, episode) & neither & 5 & \textbf{34.38} & \textbf{17.16} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_v2
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additional conditions.} Dense reward, REINFORCE++, and discount-factor sweep experiments all confirm that sign misattribution, not reward sparsity or discounting, drives the failure (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.} Varying window size reveals a credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ that separates collapse from recovery (Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}b; single seed). Below this threshold, all conditions collapse to 0\%; above it, success rate increases monotonically with window size. This single-seed observation suggests a threshold effect; multi-seed verification is needed to confirm its location and robustness.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1
\label{sec:results_hsweep}

\subsection{Sign Misattribution Verification}
\label{sec:sign_verification}

We distinguish three metrics that each capture a different aspect of credit assignment quality:

\textit{(1) Sign-episode correlation}: whether advantage sign tracks episode outcome (success/failure). Under pure binary reward (penalty${=}0$), this correlation is near-perfect at 99.6\% ($270/271$ non-zero windows across three evaluation iterations; Figure~\ref{fig:sign_misattribution}), directly confirming M2: advantage sign is almost entirely determined by episode outcome, not local action quality.\footnote{The single discordant window (1/271) arises when a low-return successful episode is grouped with higher-return successes; GRPO normalization assigns negative advantage despite positive return. This is a group-composition edge case, not a violation of the theoretical mechanism.} This is by construction under binary reward, since all windows in a successful episode share the same positive return.% exp_id: binary_credit_agreement_iter0

\textit{(2) Credit agreement rate}: whether advantage sign matches local action quality, operationalized through action validity as a proxy for local correctness (Definition~1). Under step-independent training with penalty reward ($h{=}2$, $n{=}3{,}000$ windows), only 52.07\% of windows with valid actions receive positive advantage, marginally above random chance ($p{=}0.025$ vs.\ 50\%; Cohen's $h = 2\arcsin\sqrt{0.5207} - 2\arcsin\sqrt{0.5} \approx 0.041$, below the 0.2 small-effect threshold), confirming that credit agreement is effectively at chance, consistent with the theoretical prediction of near-random sign assignment. This near-random credit agreement shows that advantage sign carries almost no information about local action quality. Under pure binary reward, credit agreement drops further to 28\% ($n{=}271$), below random chance---advantage sign systematically punishes valid actions in failed episodes. Penalty reward partially breaks sign-episode correlation by introducing within-group variance, but the resulting signal remains too weak for meaningful credit assignment. This operationalization is conservative: successful episodes may contain suboptimal windows, making our validity label an upper bound on true local correctness. Even under this generous criterion, credit agreement remains near-random, strengthening the diagnosis.% exp_id: 7b_cell_a_h2

\textit{(3) $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update})$}: the theoretical upper bound from Proposition~2 ($\leq$19.1\% at $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$). This bounds the fraction of non-zero gradient updates with correct sign, a different quantity from the credit agreement rate, which conditions on action validity rather than gradient participation. The two metrics are complementary: Proposition~2 bounds worst-case gradient quality; credit agreement measures empirical signal strength.

Combined with $\gamma{=}1.0$ collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}), these measurements are consistent with sign misattribution as a primary factor in training failure, though the credit agreement effect is substantively small ($+$2pp above random).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_sign_misattribution.pdf}
\caption{Sign-episode correlation (binary reward, penalty${=}0$) vs.\ credit agreement rate (penalty reward). Under binary reward, advantage sign near-perfectly tracks episode outcome (99.6\%, $270/271$), confirming M2. Under penalty reward, credit agreement with local action quality drops to 52.1\% ($n{=}3{,}000$), near random chance (dashed line). These are distinct metrics: the former measures episode-level sign correlation, the latter measures local credit assignment quality.}
\label{fig:sign_misattribution}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Episode-Level Training: Seed Variability}
\label{sec:seed_variability}

Episode-level GRPO addresses the dual-mechanism failure, but training outcomes remain seed-dependent. We systematically evaluate $N{=}11$ random seeds under identical hyperparameters, revealing a $2{\times}2$ structure: \textit{training stability} (collapsed vs.\ non-collapsed) $\times$ \textit{OOD generalization} (stagnant vs.\ generalized).

We define \textbf{collapsed} seeds as those where in-distribution success declines $>$50\% from peak and remains below baseline for $\geq$3 consecutive steps. \textbf{Non-collapsed} seeds are the complement. Among non-collapsed seeds, we further distinguish \textbf{generalized} (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT on the 134-task OOD set) from \textbf{stagnant} (not significant vs.\ SFT). Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} reports results for each seed at its best-performing checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Episode-level GRPO across 11 seeds (134 OOD tasks, $\tau{=}0.4$, best checkpoint per seed). Category: C=Collapsed, S=Non-collapsed (stagnant), G=Generalized (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT). Wilson 95\% CI. SFT baseline: 6.72\% (9/134). Seed2 achieves $p{=}0.03$ (Fisher exact, two-sided), falling outside the conservative $p{<}0.01$ threshold, and is classified as Stagnant. Step-5 results for all seeds in Appendix~\ref{app:step5_unified}. Evaluation variance analysis (repeated runs, std$=$1.29pp) in Appendix~\ref{app:eval_variance}.}
\label{tab:seed_variability}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Step} & \textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{vs SFT} \\
\midrule
seed2048$^\ddagger$ & 5 & C & 8.21 & [4.6, 14.2] & NS \\
seed42$^\ddagger$ & 5 & C & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed8 & 5 & C & 11.94 & [7.5, 18.5] & NS \\
\midrule
seed200 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed300 & 5 & S & 10.45 & [6.3, 16.8] & NS \\
seed100 & 5 & S & 11.19 & [6.9, 17.6] & NS \\
seed100 & 10 & S & 12.69 & [8.0, 19.4] & NS \\
seed2$^\ddagger$ & 15 & S & 15.67 & [10.5, 22.8] & $p{=}0.03$ \\
\midrule
seed1 & 5 & G$^\dagger$ & 23.88 & [17.4, 31.8] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed5 & 10 & G & 29.10 & [22.1, 37.3] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed6 & 15 & G & 29.10 & [22.1, 37.3] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
seed4 & 15 & G & 30.60 & [23.5, 38.7] & $p{<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\begin{flushleft}
\small $^\dagger$ RAGEN built-in eval; checkpoint unavailable for re-evaluation. \\
\small $^\ddagger$ OOD evaluated via RAGEN built-in eval; not independently re-evaluated under D134 protocol. \\
\small All results are single-run evaluations at $\tau{=}0.4$; see Limitations for eval variance discussion.
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}

Three distinct outcomes emerge (Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability}): roughly a quarter of seeds collapse with response length inflation (Appendix~\ref{app:response_length}), a third are stagnant with OOD performance indistinguishable from SFT, and a third generalize with significant OOD improvement. No collapsed seed generalizes, and training duration does not explain the gap between stagnant and generalized seeds. Stable training is necessary but not sufficient; the mechanism driving seed-dependent transfer remains an open question (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).% exp_id: exp029 multi-seed series

\subsection{Cross-Scale and Cross-Environment Verification}
\label{sec:cross_scale}

A single-seed experiment on Qwen3-8B provides preliminary evidence that both failure and fix persist at scale. Cell~A collapses within two steps; Cell~D reaches 90.3\% OOD, substantially exceeding the SFT baseline (full results in Appendix~\ref{app:8b_results}). Restricting context to $h{=}2$ produces a large M1 gap, confirming that information loss remains severe at scale. Single-seed due to memory constraints. % exp_id: 8b_cell_c_episode_v9d, 8b_v9d_ood_eval_v5
Step-independent training also collapses on ScienceWorld~\cite{wang2022scienceworld}: Cell~A drops to 0\% throughout all 15 steps. Restricting context to $h{=}2$ produces a comparable M1 gap to the 8B ALFWorld setting, suggesting the pathology is environment-general.% exp_id: scienceworld_grpo_cell_a, sw_info_loss_bjb1

